    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A ceremonial city at about 3,850 metres near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a>, Tiwanaku dominated the southern Andes between about 500 and 1000.</li><li>Raised fields, llama caravans and colonies in warm valleys sustained it, while its Staff God imagery spread across the southern Andes.</li><li>The city was abandoned around 1010–1050; recent radiocarbon dating suggests this came before the great drought of the 1100s.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Divers off the Island of the Sun in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-titicaca">Lake Titicaca</a> have recovered Tiwanaku offerings of puma-shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> burners and the bones of sacrificed young llamas.</p></aside>
    </section>

